#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""kb-verify — re-run the assertions that defend stale facts. Reports; applies only when told.

Every other check in this engine proves STRUCTURE: frontmatter, links, scope, that
the oracle is well formed. None of them asks the only question that matters about a
fact — is it still true? This asks it, for the narrow set of facts a command can
actually decide, and is honest about how narrow that is.

A section carries an assertion beneath its stamp (§7):

    Verified: 2026-09-19 · by: agent · method: manual
    Recheck: env -i HOME=$HOME PATH=/usr/bin:/bin bash -lc '[ -z "${KB_ENGINE_ROOT:-}" ]'

Exit 0 means the fact still holds. Non-zero means it does not.

THREE RULES, and they are the design:

  1. A PASS refreshes nothing unless you pass --apply. A verifier that restamps by
     default turns an assertion nobody re-read into a freshness machine.
  2. A FAILURE is never applied, with or without --apply. The fact is not edited, not
     marked CONFLICTED, not deleted. Something changed and a human decides what: a
     stale fact, a wrong assertion, or a real contradiction (§8). Automating that
     decision destroys the evidence that it needed making.
  3. COMMANDS ARE PRINTED AND APPROVED PER RUN (--yes). A library is content that
     travels between people; running its assertions executes its author's shell as
     you. That is a different trust question from cloning it for its facts, so it is
     asked out loud rather than assumed.

Phase 5: a FAILURE is also written to the owning library's `needs-attention.md` queue —
detection output, not a fact edit, so rule 2 still holds. The queue entry records the
file, section, assertion and the stamp it carried; a human triages it from there (§8's
"never automated: resolving a dispute"). A fact that passes later does not resolve the
entry — nothing but a human does. This reuses kb-watch's queue wholesale: same file
location, frontmatter template, escaping and dedupe-by-id, imported from
`scripts/kb-watch` rather than re-specified, so the two writers of one file can never
drift apart. See the queue section below for the stable-ID scheme and what makes a
failure merely FAILED versus unreachable.

THE ENGINE PATH (issue #60). An assertion runs from its fact file's directory, with
KB_ENGINE_ROOT set to the engine this script belongs to, overriding any value the caller
had. A library may be registered anywhere (ADR-0014), so an assertion that tests an engine
script names it as "$KB_ENGINE_ROOT/scripts/<name>"; a relative `../../scripts` reaches the
engine only while the library sits at <engine>/kb/<name>, and elsewhere fails as though the
fact were stale. Such an assertion is reported on every run, statically, as OUTSIDE: a
relative path climbing above the library root. A warning, not a failure.

ASSERTIONS THAT CANNOT FAIL (§7, issues #34 and #41) are reported on every run, statically,
whether or not they are due: a page grepped for a few words (its title or subject), or one
(url, fragment) pair backing more than two sections or sections in several files. Each passes
while the page exists and tests nothing about its own claim. A warning, not a failure.

Selection is by staleness, per §7: a section is due when its stamp is older than the
domain's freshness_horizon, or, for an agent stamp, older than its agent_ttl (a TENTH of
the horizon unless the domain declares one). The domain's verifier_budget caps how many run per sweep, oldest first.

THE QUEUE (Phase 5, plan §6 exit: "stale or drifted facts surface as a queue rather
than rotting silently"): on every FAILED assertion run under --yes, kb-verify appends
one record to `<root>/needs-attention.md` — reusing kb-watch's queue exactly (it is
imported as a module at `scripts/kb-watch`, not re-implemented): the same file
location, the same frontmatter template (`new_queue_file`), the same escaping
(`yaml_scalar`: untrusted values flattened, fence-breaking backtick runs neutralised,
truncated to 200 characters), and the same dedupe-by-id lookup (`existing_ids`). Only
the record's own fields and the stable-ID formula differ, because what a Recheck
failure needs to say (which file, which section, what assertion, what stamp it
carried) is not what a Watcher drift needs to say (which field, old value, new value) —
reusing kb-watch's own `render_record`/`append_records` would mean forcing this
record into that shape, which is matching the format by accident rather than by
design, so those two small pieces are written fresh here, against the shared
primitives, for this record shape:

    category: stale-recheck        the assertion ran and returned false
    category: stale-recheck-error  the command itself could not be evaluated as
                                    true/false — distinguished where the shell's exit
                                    code says so unambiguously (126 "not executable",
                                    127 "not found") or the run timed out; an ordinary
                                    nonzero exit from a command that *ran* is not this —
                                    a shell exit code cannot otherwise tell "the
                                    assertion is false" apart from "the assertion
                                    command is broken," and this says so rather than
                                    pretending finer detection exists
    status: open                   always, on write; a human (never this script)
                                    moves it to acknowledged/resolved
    file, section                  where the fact lives, library-relative
    assertion                      the Recheck: command that failed
    stamp                          the Verified: line it carried when it failed
    exit                           the observed exit detail, for a human's triage

STABLE ID: 8 hex chars of sha1(f"{category}|{file}|{section}|{assertion}") — the
file/section/assertion identify *which claim* failed; category is included (as
kb-watch's own ID does) so a plain failure and a command-error finding for the same
claim can never collide. Re-running on the same still-failing assertion reproduces the
same id and is not re-added (dedupe) — including across runs that do not pass --apply,
since a failure is never restamped and so looks identical to the verifier every time it
is not fixed. A fact that later passes is not auto-resolved and is not re-queued either
(nothing to re-raise — it is no longer failing); an existing open entry for it is left
for a human, per rule 2.

Usage: kb-verify <library-or-tree> [--yes] [--apply] [--all] [--budget N]
  --yes      run the commands (without it, they are printed and nothing runs, and
             nothing is queued either — the --yes gate covers both)
  --apply    restamp sections whose assertion passed (today, by: agent, method: query)
  --all      every section with an assertion, not only the stale ones
  --budget N override the domain's verifier_budget
A FAILED assertion (only ever reachable with --yes) is queued to the owning library's
needs-attention.md — see THE QUEUE above. No separate flag gates this: it is detection
output, not a mutation of any fact (documents/state-change-gates.md), so it follows
--yes exactly the way printing PASS/FAIL already does, and no --no-queue or --dry-run
exists because there is nothing for it to opt out of that --yes does not already gate.
Exit 0 when nothing failed, 1 when an assertion failed, 2 on usage error.
"""
import datetime, hashlib, importlib.machinery, importlib.util, os, pathlib, re, shlex, subprocess, sys

NON_KB = {"documents", "plugins", "scripts", ".github", ".githooks",
          ".claude-plugin", ".claude", ".git"}
STAMP = re.compile(r"^Verified:\s*(\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2})\s*·\s*by:\s*(\S+)\s*·\s*method:\s*(\S+)\s*$")
RECHECK = re.compile(r"^Recheck:\s*(.+?)\s*$")
HEADING = re.compile(r"^(#{1,6})\s+(.*)$")
URL = re.compile(r"https?://[^\s'\"|;)<>]+")
SHORT_FRAGMENT = 4      # words; 26 of 31 fragments in the #34 sample were 1-4 words long
REUSE_LIMIT = 2         # sections one (url, fragment) pair may back, all in one file (#41)
TODAY = datetime.date.today()
ENGINE = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
CLIMB = re.compile(r"(?<![\w.])((?:\.\./)+|(?:\.\./)*\.\.(?![\w./]))")


# ---------------------------------------------------------- the needs-attention queue ----
def load_kb_watch():
    """Import scripts/kb-watch as a module, byte-identical to the file on disk — not a
    copy. kb-watch has no .py suffix (it is invoked as a script, like this file), so this
    is the one way to call its functions directly instead of re-specifying them: the
    queue's frontmatter template, its escaping, and its dedupe lookup live in exactly one
    place, and this script and kb-watch share it rather than risk drifting apart."""
    path = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent / "kb-watch"
    # kb-watch has no .py suffix, so spec_from_file_location cannot infer a loader from
    # the extension (it returns None) — an explicit SourceFileLoader is required. That
    # loader otherwise caches bytecode to a scripts/__pycache__/ next to it, same as a
    # normal import — an engine script that is supposed to write only the queue should
    # not also litter its own directory, so bytecode caching is turned off first.
    sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("kb_watch", str(path))
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(loader.name, loader)
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    loader.exec_module(mod)
    return mod


def load_check_kb():
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(
        "check_kb", str(pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent / "check-kb"))
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(loader.name, loader)
    check_kb = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
    loader.exec_module(check_kb)
    return check_kb


def move_floor(path: pathlib.Path):
    """Rewrite a declared frontmatter verified: to the floor the file's stamps now support. A
    restamp can raise the oldest stamp, and check-kb fails a floor that disagrees with its
    stamps (§7) — so the derivation is check-kb's own, imported, not a second copy of it."""
    check_kb = load_check_kb()
    text = path.read_text()
    parts = text.split("---\n", 2)
    if not text.startswith("---\n") or len(parts) < 3:
        return
    floor = check_kb.derived_floor(parts[2])
    fm = re.sub(r"^verified:[ \t]*\S+", f"verified: {floor}", parts[1], count=1, flags=re.M)
    path.write_text("---\n" + fm + "---\n" + parts[2])


def stable_id(category: str, file: str, section: str, assertion: str) -> str:
    payload = f"{category}|{file}|{section}|{assertion}".encode()
    return hashlib.sha1(payload).hexdigest()[:8]


def failure_detail(result):
    """(category, exit-detail) for one failed assertion. 126/127 are the shell's own
    signal that the command itself could not be run (not executable / not found) —
    distinct from a command that ran and evaluated false. `result` is None on a timeout,
    which is the same kind of finding: the assertion was never actually evaluated."""
    if result is None:
        return "stale-recheck-error", "timed out after 120s"
    if result.returncode in (126, 127):
        return "stale-recheck-error", f"exit {result.returncode} (command not found or not executable)"
    return "stale-recheck", f"exit {result.returncode}"


def render_queue_record(rec: dict, kb_watch) -> str:
    """Same record shape kb-watch writes (a '- id: ...' header, two-space-indented
    'key: value' lines, the fenced ```yaml block) and the same escaping (`yaml_scalar`),
    but this record's own fields — kb-watch's render_record is hardwired to its own
    (source_id/field/old/new/source_url), so reusing it verbatim would mean forcing a
    Recheck failure into a Watcher drift's shape instead of describing what actually
    failed."""
    lines = [f"- id: {rec['id']}"]
    for key in ("category", "status", "file"):
        lines.append(f"  {key}: {kb_watch.yaml_scalar(rec[key], untrusted=False)}")
    for key in ("section", "assertion", "stamp"):
        lines.append(f"  {key}: {kb_watch.yaml_scalar(rec[key], untrusted=True)}")
    for key in ("exit", "date"):
        lines.append(f"  {key}: {kb_watch.yaml_scalar(rec[key], untrusted=False)}")
    return "\n".join(lines) + "\n\n"


def append_queue_records(queue_path: pathlib.Path, records: list, kb_watch):
    """Byte-for-byte the same file-level behaviour as kb-watch's append_records: create
    the file from kb-watch's own frontmatter template if absent, otherwise splice into
    the last ```yaml fence. Duplicated rather than called — kb-watch's version calls its
    own render_record internally, so reusing it would hit the same shape mismatch as
    above — but every primitive it uses here (FENCE_OPEN/CLOSE, new_queue_file,
    library_domain_scope) is kb-watch's own, imported, not re-specified."""
    body = "".join(render_queue_record(r, kb_watch) for r in records)
    if not queue_path.is_file():
        domain, scope, err = kb_watch.library_domain_scope(queue_path.parent)
        if err:
            return err
        kb_watch.atomic_write(
            queue_path, kb_watch.new_queue_file(domain, scope)[:-len(kb_watch.FENCE_CLOSE)] + body + kb_watch.FENCE_CLOSE)
        return kb_watch.ensure_routed(queue_path.parent)   # an unrouted queue fails check-kb (CONVENTIONS §3)
    text = queue_path.read_text(errors="replace")
    spans = list(re.finditer(r"```yaml\n(?:.*?\n)?```\n", text, re.S))
    if spans:
        last = spans[-1]
        insert_at = last.end() - len(kb_watch.FENCE_CLOSE)
        text = text[:insert_at] + body + text[insert_at:]
    else:
        text = text.rstrip("\n") + "\n\n" + kb_watch.FENCE_OPEN + body + kb_watch.FENCE_CLOSE
    kb_watch.atomic_write(queue_path, text)
    return None


def horizon_and_budget(root: pathlib.Path):
    idx = (root / "INDEX.md").read_text(errors="replace")
    h = re.search(r"^freshness_horizon:\s*(\d+)d", idx, re.M)
    b = re.search(r"^verifier_budget:\s*(\d+)", idx, re.M)
    return (int(h.group(1)) if h else None), (int(b.group(1)) if b else None)


def agent_ttl(root: pathlib.Path, horizon: int) -> int:
    """The domain's agent-stamp TTL: its agent_ttl:, else a tenth of the horizon (§7), by
    check-kb's own rule. Raises ValueError on one check-kb would fail."""
    idx = (root / "INDEX.md").read_text(errors="replace")
    m = re.search(r"^agent_ttl:[ \t]*(.*?)[ \t]*$", idx, re.M)
    return load_check_kb().agent_ttl(m.group(1) if m else None, horizon)


def is_due(s: dict, horizon: int, agent_ttl: int) -> bool:
    """A stamped section is due for recheck once its age exceeds its trust tier's TTL —
    the domain's agent TTL for an agent stamp, the full horizon for a human one (§7).
    Top-level (not nested in main()) so kb-due can import and call it: the two scripts must
    judge staleness identically, and a condition copied into a second file is a condition
    that can drift out from under the first."""
    age = (TODAY - s["date"]).days
    return age > (agent_ttl if s["by"] == "agent" else horizon)


def kb_markdown(root: pathlib.Path):
    """The tree's knowledge files, sorted. Excluded directories are PRUNED during the walk,
    not filtered after it: rglob would descend into .git and the archived evidence, and on a
    slow filesystem (a Windows drive under WSL) that walk alone took seconds on every session
    start, since kb-due's nudge calls this from the SessionStart hook."""
    out = []
    for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(root):
        dirnames[:] = [d for d in dirnames if d not in NON_KB]
        out += [pathlib.Path(dirpath) / n for n in filenames
                if n.endswith(".md") and not n.endswith("-golden.md")]
    return sorted(out)


def due_sections(root: pathlib.Path, horizon: int, agent_ttl: int, all_sections=False):
    """(due_sorted, found, with_cmd): every stamped section in the tree (`found`), the ones
    that carry a Recheck: assertion (`with_cmd`), and the due subset — oldest stamp first,
    NOT yet capped to a budget (`due_sorted`). Exposed so kb-due's nudge can report the same
    'due to run' set this script would select by default, without re-deriving staleness."""
    found = []
    for f in kb_markdown(root):
        found += sections(f)
    with_cmd = [s for s in found if s["cmd"]]
    due = [s for s in with_cmd if all_sections or is_due(s, horizon, agent_ttl)]
    due.sort(key=lambda s: s["date"])
    return due, found, with_cmd


def sections(path: pathlib.Path):
    """Every stamped section: its heading, stamp line number, date, by, and assertion."""
    lines = path.read_text(errors="replace").split("\n")
    heading, out = "(file)", []
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        m = HEADING.match(line)
        if m:
            heading = m.group(2).strip()
            continue
        s = STAMP.match(line)
        if not s:
            continue
        cmd = None
        for nxt in lines[i + 1:i + 3]:          # the assertion sits directly beneath the stamp
            r = RECHECK.match(nxt)
            if r:
                cmd = r.group(1); break
            if nxt.strip():
                break
        out.append({"file": path, "heading": heading, "line": i,
                    "date": datetime.date.fromisoformat(s.group(1)),
                    "by": s.group(2), "method": s.group(3), "cmd": cmd})
    return out


def last_grep(cmd: str):
    """(options, pattern) of the command's last grep, or None when there is no grep or its
    arguments cannot be read. Options are the tokens before the pattern; -e names the pattern
    explicitly."""
    m = list(re.finditer(r"\bgrep\b", cmd))
    if not m:
        return None
    lex = shlex.shlex(cmd[m[-1].end():], posix=True, punctuation_chars=True)
    lex.whitespace_split = True
    try:
        toks = list(lex)
    except ValueError:
        return None
    opts = []
    for i, tok in enumerate(toks):
        if tok in ("|", "||", "&&", ";", "&", ">", "<"):
            return None
        if tok == "-e":
            return (opts, toks[i + 1]) if i + 1 < len(toks) else None
        if not tok.startswith("-"):
            return opts, tok
        opts.append(tok)
    return None


def grep_fragment(cmd: str):
    """The string the command's last grep looks for, or None (see last_grep)."""
    g = last_grep(cmd)
    return g[1] if g else None


def grep_mode(options: list) -> str:
    """F (fixed strings), E (extended), P (Perl) or G (basic, grep's default); the last
    flag given wins, as in grep."""
    mode = "G"
    long = {"--fixed-strings": "F", "--extended-regexp": "E", "--perl-regexp": "P", "--basic-regexp": "G"}
    for o in options:
        if o in long:
            mode = long[o]
        elif o.startswith("-") and not o.startswith("--"):
            for c in o[1:]:
                if c in "FEPG":
                    mode = c
    return mode


TAG = re.compile(r"<[^<>]*>")
SEP = "\x00"


def text_runs(pattern: str, mode: str) -> list:
    """For each alternative of the pattern, the runs of literal text it requires, split by
    markup tags and, outside -F, by regex syntax (issue #65). A run must hold a letter or
    digit to count. Under -F every character is literal, so only tags split it."""
    if mode == "F":
        alts = [pattern]
    else:
        alt_bar = "\\|" if mode == "G" else "|"
        alts, depth, cur, i = [], 0, "", 0
        while i < len(pattern):
            c = pattern[i]
            if c == "\\" and i + 1 < len(pattern):
                if pattern[i:i + 2] == alt_bar and depth == 0:
                    alts.append(cur); cur = ""; i += 2; continue
                cur += pattern[i:i + 2]; i += 2; continue
            if c in "([":
                depth += 1
            elif c in ")]" and depth:
                depth -= 1
            elif c == "|" and alt_bar == "|" and depth == 0:
                alts.append(cur); cur = ""; i += 1; continue
            cur += c; i += 1
        alts.append(cur)
    # A group holding an alternation requires none of its branches in particular, so it
    # contributes no run: `(DENY|Deny)` must not count as two.
    if mode == "G":
        grouped_alt = re.compile(r"\\\((?:(?!\\[()]).)*?\\\|(?:(?!\\[()]).)*?\\\)")
    else:
        grouped_alt = re.compile(r"\((?:[^()\\]|\\.)*?\|(?:[^()\\]|\\.)*?\)")
    out = []
    for alt in alts:
        if mode != "F":
            while grouped_alt.search(alt):
                alt = grouped_alt.sub(SEP, alt)
            meta = ".*[^$" + ("" if mode == "G" else "()+?{}|")
            lit, i = "", 0
            while i < len(alt):
                c = alt[i]
                if c == "\\" and i + 1 < len(alt):
                    n = alt[i + 1]
                    lit += SEP if n.isalnum() or (mode == "G" and n in "(){}+?|") else n
                    i += 2; continue
                if c == "[":
                    j = alt.find("]", i + 2)
                    lit += SEP; i = (j + 1) if j != -1 else len(alt); continue
                lit += SEP if c in meta else c
                i += 1
            alt = lit
        runs = [r for r in re.split(r"<[^<>]*>|\x00", alt) if re.search(r"[A-Za-z0-9]", r)]
        out.append(runs)
    return out

def weak_rechecks(root: pathlib.Path, sections: list) -> list:
    """Doc-review assertions that cannot fail (§7, issues #34 and #41): a page fetched and
    grepped for a fragment of a few words (its title, its subject, an identifier), or one
    (url, fragment) pair backing many sections or sections in several files. Either passes
    for as long as the page exists, which says nothing about the claim. Static — nothing is
    fetched or run — and a warning, never a failure: a heuristic must not block a sweep.
    Only commands that name a URL and grep it are judged; a local query is left alone.
    A short pattern that binds two runs of text — split by markup, or outside -F by regex —
    tests a relation (a field and its value), not a title, and is not flagged; an
    alternation is as weak as its weakest branch (issue #65)."""
    out, pairs = [], {}
    for s in sections:
        url, frag = URL.search(s["cmd"] or ""), grep_fragment(s["cmd"] or "")
        if not (url and frag):
            continue
        where = f"{s['file'].relative_to(root)} § {s['heading']}"
        words = len(frag.split())
        bound = all(len(runs) > 1 for runs in text_runs(frag, grep_mode(last_grep(s["cmd"])[0])))
        if words <= SHORT_FRAGMENT and not bound:
            out.append(f"WEAK {where}: greps {words} word{'s' if words != 1 else ''} ('{frag[:60]}') — "
                       f"a title or identifier passes while the page exists")
        pairs.setdefault((url.group(0), frag), []).append((s["file"], where))
    for (url, frag), backs in pairs.items():
        files = {f for f, _ in backs}
        if len(backs) > REUSE_LIMIT or len(files) > 1:
            out.append(f"WEAK one fragment backs {len(backs)} sections in {len(files)} "
                       f"file{'s' if len(files) != 1 else ''} ('{frag[:60]}' at {url}): "
                       + "; ".join(w for _, w in backs)
                       + " — it cannot test each section's own claim")
    return out


def outside_rechecks(root: pathlib.Path, sections: list) -> list:
    """Assertions whose relative path climbs above the library root (issue #60). Static, a
    warning: the path resolves only in one layout, and elsewhere the fact reads as stale."""
    out = []
    for s in sections:
        depth = len(s["file"].parent.relative_to(root).parts)
        if any(m.group(1).count("..") > depth for m in CLIMB.finditer(s["cmd"] or "")):
            out.append(f"OUTSIDE {s['file'].relative_to(root)} § {s['heading']}: a relative path climbs "
                       f"out of the library — name the engine as \"$KB_ENGINE_ROOT/scripts/...\"")
    return out


def main(argv) -> int:
    args = [a for i, a in enumerate(argv)
            if not a.startswith("--") and (i == 0 or argv[i - 1] != "--budget")]
    flags = {a for a in argv if a.startswith("--")}
    if len(args) != 1 or flags - {"--yes", "--apply", "--all", "--budget"}:
        print(__doc__.strip().split("\n\n")[-1], file=sys.stderr); return 2
    root = pathlib.Path(args[0])
    if not (root / "INDEX.md").is_file():
        print(f"kb-verify: {root} has no INDEX.md — not a library or project tree", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    budget_override = None
    if "--budget" in flags:
        try:
            budget_override = int(argv[argv.index("--budget") + 1])
        except (ValueError, IndexError):
            print("kb-verify: --budget needs a number", file=sys.stderr); return 2

    horizon, budget = horizon_and_budget(root)
    if horizon is None:
        print(f"kb-verify: {root}/INDEX.md declares no freshness_horizon — nothing to judge staleness by",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    budget = budget_override or budget or 5
    try:
        ttl = agent_ttl(root, horizon)
    except ValueError as e:
        print(f"kb-verify: {root}/INDEX.md: {e}", file=sys.stderr); return 2

    due, found, with_cmd = due_sections(root, horizon, ttl, "--all" in flags)
    stamped = len(found)
    over_budget = max(0, len(due) - budget)
    selected = due[:budget]

    print(f"kb-verify: {root}  (horizon {horizon}d, agent TTL {ttl}d, budget {budget})")
    print(f"  {stamped} stamped section(s), {len(with_cmd)} with a Recheck assertion, "
          f"{len(selected)} due to run"
          + (f", {over_budget} more past the budget" if over_budget else ""))

    weak = weak_rechecks(root, with_cmd)
    if weak:
        print(f"\n  Assertions that cannot fail — grep each claim's own supporting sentence (§7) ({len(weak)}):")
        for w in weak:
            print(f"    {w}")

    outside = outside_rechecks(root, with_cmd)
    if outside:
        print(f"\n  Assertions that reach outside the library — they hold in one layout only ({len(outside)}):")
        for o in outside:
            print(f"    {o}")

    # The facts no command can settle. Naming them is the point: a verifier that
    # only reports what it can check reads as though everything else were verified.
    unverifiable = [s for s in found if not s["cmd"] and is_due(s, horizon, ttl)]
    if unverifiable:
        print(f"\n  STALE, no assertion — a human or an agent must re-check these ({len(unverifiable)}):")
        for s in unverifiable:
            print(f"    {s['file'].relative_to(root)} § {s['heading']}"
                  f"  ({(TODAY - s['date']).days}d, by: {s['by']})")

    if not selected:
        print("\nNothing to run.")
        return 0

    print(f"\n  Commands{'' if '--yes' in flags else ' (NOT run — pass --yes to run them)'}:")
    for s in selected:
        print(f"    {s['file'].relative_to(root)} § {s['heading']}  ({(TODAY - s['date']).days}d)")
        print(f"      $ {s['cmd']}")
    if "--yes" not in flags:
        print("\nRead them before you do. A library's assertions run as you, with your privileges.")
        return 0

    passed, failed = [], []
    print()
    for s in selected:
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(s["cmd"], shell=True, cwd=s["file"].parent,
                               env={**os.environ, "KB_ENGINE_ROOT": str(ENGINE)},
                               capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            r = None
        where = f"{s['file'].relative_to(root)} § {s['heading']}"
        if r is not None and r.returncode == 0:
            passed.append(s); print(f"  PASS {where}")
        else:
            failed.append((s, r))
            print(f"  FAIL {where}  (exit {'timeout' if r is None else r.returncode})")
            # What the assertion printed can be the source's own text. It goes to a person at a
            # terminal, never into the log of an unattended run (#95).
            if r is not None and sys.stdout.isatty():
                for stream in (r.stdout, r.stderr):
                    for line in stream.strip().split("\n")[:3]:
                        if line:
                            print(f"         {line}")

    if passed and "--apply" in flags:
        for s in passed:
            lines = s["file"].read_text().split("\n")
            lines[s["line"]] = f"Verified: {TODAY} · by: agent · method: query"
            s["file"].write_text("\n".join(lines))
        for f in {s["file"] for s in passed}:
            move_floor(f)
        print(f"\n  restamped {len(passed)} section(s) to {TODAY} (by: agent — kb-verify is an agent, "
              f"whatever ran the assertion)")
    elif passed:
        print(f"\n  {len(passed)} section(s) still hold. Not restamped — pass --apply for that.")

    if failed:
        print(f"\n  {len(failed)} assertion(s) FAILED. Nothing was edited, deliberately: the fact may be")
        print( "  stale, the assertion may be wrong, or the world may have changed and the fact is now")
        print( "  a contradiction (§8). That is a human's call, and auto-editing destroys the evidence.")

        kb_watch = load_kb_watch()
        queue_path = root / "needs-attention.md"
        seen = kb_watch.existing_ids(queue_path)
        to_write = []
        for s, r in failed:
            category, detail = failure_detail(r)
            file_rel = str(s["file"].relative_to(root))
            section = s["heading"]
            assertion = s["cmd"]
            stamp = f"{s['date']} · by: {s['by']} · method: {s['method']}"
            rid = stable_id(category, file_rel, section, assertion)
            if rid in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(rid)
            to_write.append({"id": rid, "category": category, "status": "open",
                             "file": file_rel, "section": section, "assertion": assertion,
                             "stamp": stamp, "exit": detail, "date": str(TODAY)})

        queued = len(to_write)
        dupes = len(failed) - queued
        if to_write:
            err = append_queue_records(queue_path, to_write, kb_watch)
            if err:
                print(f"\n  kb-verify: could not queue to {queue_path}: {err}", file=sys.stderr)
            else:
                print(f"\n  queued {queued} new finding(s) to {queue_path}"
                      + (f" ({dupes} already queued)" if dupes else ""))
        elif dupes:
            print(f"\n  {dupes} failure(s) already queued in {queue_path} — not re-added.")
    return 1 if failed else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
